---
import {
  Analytics01Icon,
  ChartLineData01Icon,
  CloudIcon,
  Calendar03Icon,
} from '@hugeicons/core-free-icons'
import FeaturePage from '@/components/FeaturePage.astro'

const related = [
  { title: 'seo report', text: 'Every section of the Search Console report and how to read it.', href: '/docs/report', icon: Analytics01Icon },
  { title: 'seo compare', text: 'Equal periods before and after a release or a Google update.', href: '/docs/compare', icon: Calendar03Icon },
  { title: 'Google Analytics', text: 'Connect Google Analytics so reports show search sessions too.', href: '/docs/analytics', icon: ChartLineData01Icon },
  { title: 'Run in cloud agents', text: 'Run reports from an agent that works on a schedule.', href: '/docs/cloud-agents', icon: CloudIcon },
]
---

<FeaturePage
  title="SEO reporting tool for AI agents"
  description="Automated SEO reports from your own Search Console and Google Analytics data. Ask your agent for a weekly or client SEO report built from real clicks, queries and sessions."
  path="/features/seo-reporting"
  lead="Ask your agent for an SEO report and it pulls the numbers from Search Console and Google Analytics first, then writes the summary you would otherwise put together by hand."
  {related}
>
  <h2>What goes into the SEO report</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it shows</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Search totals</td><td>Clicks, impressions, click-through rate and average position against the previous period, by device, with the top pages and queries and how they changed.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Google Analytics</td><td>Organic Search sessions from Google and from every search engine, sessions from all channels, and visits from AI assistants, for the same dates.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Opportunities</td><td>Quick wins, titles with a low click-through rate, queries just off page one and pages on page two.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Problems</td><td>Content that lost clicks, queries where your own pages compete, and sudden traffic drops with the Google updates near them.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Before and after</td><td>Equal periods either side of a release or an update, for the whole site or a set of pages.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The report returns text and structured data. Your agent turns it into
    the document you want, whether that is a short update for your team
    or a monthly report for a client.
  </p>

  <h2>Run a report for one site</h2>
  <p>Ask your agent:</p>
  <pre><code>Run the SEO report for example.com</code></pre>
  <p>In a terminal, with the CLI installed:</p>
  <pre><code>seo report --site example.com</code></pre>
  <p>
    It covers the last 28 days of final Search Console data unless you give
    other dates. Add <code>--section content-decay</code> (or any other
    section) to open one part, and <code>--json</code> when a script or
    agent reads the result.
  </p>

  <h2>Write a client SEO report</h2>
  <p>
    Link each Google account that can read a client's Search Console
    property on your <a href="/account">account page</a>.
    Every request uses the linked account that can read that site, so one
    agent can report on all of your clients. Then give it the month:
  </p>
  <pre><code>Write the September 2026 SEO report for example.com. Use seo report
for 2026-09-01 to 2026-09-30, then the content-decay and quick-wins
sections. Keep every number as reported and say where data is missing.</code></pre>
  <p>
    SEO Skill gives your agent the facts. The layout, the branding and the
    advice come from you and your agent, in whatever document you already
    send. Keep Search Console clicks and Google Analytics sessions under
    their own names, because they are different counts and a client will
    ask why they do not match.
  </p>

  <h2>Automated SEO reports each week</h2>
  <p>
    A scheduled agent task can run the report every Monday and send you a
    summary. Keep the same length of date window each time, so this week
    compares fairly with the last. The <a href="/docs/cloud-agents">cloud
    agents guide</a> shows how to give a scheduled agent your account.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sites in the weekly site audit (a beta, $50 a month per site) also get a
    Monday email and webhook with each site's week: Google index checks,
    what the weekly crawl found and search clicks against the week before.
    Running <code>seo report</code> with no site returns the same summary
    across all of your audited sites.
  </p>

  <h2>Check the numbers before you send them</h2>
  <p>
    The click-through rate curve comes from your own site's data, so it is a
    useful yardstick and not a Google rule. When a traffic drop lines up
    with a Google update, the report shows that the dates overlap. It does
    not prove the update caused the drop, so the client report should not
    say so either.
  </p>
</FeaturePage>
